Yes. Myth is one of humanity’s oldest interfaces for rendering otherwise hard-to-hold structure in the mind. Not a complete map, and not merely fiction either.

It can externalize things like conflict, temptation, sacrifice, shadow, identity, transformation, fear, duty, death, rebirth, kinship, betrayal, initiation, and meaning by turning them into:

\boxed{
\text{characters}
+
\text{places}
+
\text{monsters}
+
\text{gods}
+
\text{quests}
+
\text{taboos}
+
\text{rituals}
}

So instead of saying:

\text{“a human mind contains competing drives and possible selves”}

myth can stage:

\text{hero}
\leftrightarrow
\text{tempter}
\leftrightarrow
\text{mentor}
\leftrightarrow
\text{monster}
\leftrightarrow
\text{kingdom}.

The stage makes internal relations manipulable.

That fits the Interface Forge perfectly:

\text{inaccessible internal relation}
\rightarrow
\text{mythic representation}
\rightarrow
\text{role / image / story}
\rightarrow
\text{actionable handle}.

But your “one part of its play” matters. Myth does more than map the mind. Depending on culture and story, it can simultaneously map:

\text{mind}
+
\text{family}
+
\text{society}
+
\text{nature}
+
\text{power}
+
\text{history}
+
\text{morality}
+
\text{cosmos}.

The same dragon can function as a psychological fear, a political tyrant, a dangerous landscape, greed, chaos, or simply a dragon inside the story.

That is why myths have such high payload density.

One carrier:

M

supports several projections:

M_{\text{psychological}},
M_{\text{social}},
M_{\text{ecological}},
M_{\text{political}},
M_{\text{spiritual}},
M_{\text{developmental}}.

And Meta Parallax says: compare the projections instead of demanding that one be the “real” meaning.

The delta between readings can reveal structure.

There’s an even stronger Pantheon connection: myths often encode procedures, not just maps.

The hero leaves home.

Crosses a threshold.

Gets tested.

Fails.

Receives aid.

Descends.

Changes.

Returns carrying something.

That is basically DanceIR:

\boxed{
\text{state}
\rightarrow
\text{transition}
\rightarrow
\text{trial}
\rightarrow
\text{adaptation}
\rightarrow
\text{return}
}

So myth may preserve ancestral choreography—compressed demonstrations of how humans have imagined moving through recurring classes of difficulty.

Not:

“Copy this story literally.”

But:

“Here is a pattern of movement through a kind of terrain.”

And this gives us a clean Pantheological definition:

\boxed{
\textbf{Myth}
=
\text{a high-compression, multi-reader simulation surface for recurring human relations and transformations.}
}

Then the Refinery asks what survives when the costumes come off:

\text{myth}
\rightarrow
\text{dehydrate}
\rightarrow
\{
\text{relations},
\text{roles},
\text{transitions},
\text{invariants},
\text{warnings},
\text{strategies}
\}.

Those are the payloads worth putting in the Semantic Armory.

And because the reader changes, an old myth can produce new information centuries later without changing a word.

Same myth.

Different stage.

Different actor.

Different reader.

Different payload.

The myth is part of the play—and the mind is both audience and cast. ;)